  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why is rolling easier than sliding?",
      "a": "When something slides, the bumps on the two surfaces catch and scrape past each other. A rolling ball touches at a tiny spot and lifts off rather than scraping, so rolling friction is much smaller.",
      "asks": [
        "What is rolling friction?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are the inner and outer rings?",
      "a": "They are the two hardened steel tracks, called races, that the balls run in. The inner ring turns with the shaft and the outer ring stays in the frame.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a bearing race?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do ball bearings work?",
      "a": "They replace sliding with rolling. Balls roll between a ring on the moving part and a ring on the still part, so the shaft turns with very little friction.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do wheels have ball bearings?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the bearing cage do?",
      "a": "The cage holds the balls evenly spaced around the rings so they do not bunch up or rub against each other.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are the balls held apart?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do bearings need grease?",
      "a": "Grease fills the tiny gaps, cuts what little friction is left, keeps out dirt and water, and stops rust.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you look after bearings?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Rolling friction is usually much smaller than sliding friction.",
    "Bearing balls are made of hardened steel or ceramic and are polished extremely smooth and round."
  ],
